{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17172","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17172","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17172","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Djamel Eddine","familyName":"Boukhari","name":"Boukhari, Djamel Eddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SynergyNet: Fusing Generative Priors and State-Space Models for Facial Beauty Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T17:36:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The automated prediction of facial beauty is a benchmark task in affective computing that requires a sophisticated understanding of both local aesthetic details (e.g., skin texture) and global facial harmony (e.g., symmetry, proportions). Existing models, based on either Convolutional Neural Networks (CNNs) or Vision Transformers (ViTs), exhibit inherent architectural biases that limit their performance; CNNs excel at local feature extraction but struggle with long-range dependencies, while ViTs model global relationships at a significant computational cost. This paper introduces the \\textbf{Mamba-Diffusion Network (MD-Net)}, a novel dual-stream architecture that resolves this trade-off by delegating specialized roles to state-of-the-art models. The first stream leverages a frozen U-Net encoder from a pre-trained latent diffusion model, providing a powerful generative prior for fine-grained aesthetic qualities. The second stream employs a Vision Mamba (Vim), a modern state-space model, to efficiently capture global facial structure with linear-time complexity. By synergistically integrating these complementary representations through a cross-attention mechanism, MD-Net creates a holistic and nuanced feature space for prediction. Evaluated on the SCUT-FBP5500 benchmark, MD-Net sets a new state-of-the-art, achieving a Pearson Correlation of \\textbf{0.9235} and demonstrating the significant potential of hybrid architectures that fuse generative and sequential modeling paradigms for complex visual assessment tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17172","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:05:44Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:05:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:05:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17105","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.17105","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.17105","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Haoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weixin","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Weixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRPOformer: Advancing Hyperparameter Optimization via Group Relative Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T14:54:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:56:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hyperparameter optimization (HPO) plays a critical role in improving model performance. Transformer-based HPO methods have shown great potential; however, existing approaches rely heavily on large-scale historical optimization trajectories and lack effective reinforcement learning (RL) techniques, thereby limiting their efficiency and performance improvements. Inspired by the success of Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) in large language models (LLMs), we propose GRPOformer -- a novel hyperparameter optimization framework that integrates reinforcement learning (RL) with Transformers. In GRPOformer, Transformers are employed to generate new hyperparameter configurations from historical optimization trajectories, while GRPO enables rapid trajectory construction and optimization strategy learning from scratch. Moreover, we introduce Policy Churn Regularization (PCR) to enhance the stability of GRPO training. Experimental results on OpenML demonstrate that GRPOformer consistently outperforms baseline methods across diverse tasks, offering new insights into the application of RL for HPO."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17105","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:04:08Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:04:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:04:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16990","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16990","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16990","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avishai","familyName":"Elmakies","name":"Elmakies, Avishai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hagai","familyName":"Aronowitz","name":"Aronowitz, Hagai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nimrod","familyName":"Shabtay","name":"Shabtay, Nimrod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eli","familyName":"Schwartz","name":"Schwartz, Eli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ron","familyName":"Hoory","name":"Hoory, Ron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Avihu","familyName":"Dekel","name":"Dekel, Avihu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advancing Speech Understanding in Speech-Aware Language Models with GRPO"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T09:09:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:50:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we introduce a Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)-based method for training Speech-Aware Large Language Models (SALLMs) on open-format speech understanding tasks, such as Spoken Question Answering and Automatic Speech Translation. SALLMs have proven highly effective for speech understanding tasks. GRPO has recently gained traction for its efficiency in training LLMs, and prior work has explored its application to SALLMs, primarily in multiple-choice tasks. Building on this, we focus on open-format tasks that better reflect the generative abilities of the models. Our approach leverages GRPO with BLEU as the reward signal to optimize SALLMs, and we demonstrate empirically that it surpasses standard SFT across several key metrics. Finally, we explore the potential of incorporating off-policy samples within GRPO for these tasks, highlighting avenues for further improvement and further research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16990","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:01:25Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:01:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:01:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minchan","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Minchan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwon","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Junwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangil","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kangil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junmo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junmo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Preference Distillation via Value based Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T07:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:48:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) is a powerful paradigm to align language models with human preferences using pairwise comparisons. However, its binary win-or-loss supervision often proves insufficient for training small models with limited capacity. Prior works attempt to distill information from large teacher models using behavior cloning or KL divergence. These methods often focus on mimicking current behavior and overlook distilling reward modeling. To address this issue, we propose \\textit{Teacher Value-based Knowledge Distillation} (TVKD), which introduces an auxiliary reward from the value function of the teacher model to provide a soft guide. This auxiliary reward is formulated to satisfy potential-based reward shaping, ensuring that the global reward structure and optimal policy of DPO are preserved. TVKD can be integrated into the standard DPO training framework and does not require additional rollouts. Our experimental results show that TVKD consistently improves performance across various benchmarks and model sizes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 page"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T03:00:49Z","registered":"2025-09-23T03:00:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T03:00:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nawar","familyName":"Turk","name":"Turk, Nawar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniele","familyName":"Comitogianni","name":"Comitogianni, Daniele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leila","familyName":"Kosseim","name":"Kosseim, Leila","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CLaC at DISRPT 2025: Hierarchical Adapters for Cross-Framework Multi-lingual Discourse Relation Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T03:34:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:44:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present our submission to Task 3 (Discourse Relation Classification) of the DISRPT 2025 shared task. Task 3 introduces a unified set of 17 discourse relation labels across 39 corpora in 16 languages and six discourse frameworks, posing significant multilingual and cross-formalism challenges. We first benchmark the task by fine-tuning multilingual BERT-based models (mBERT, XLM-RoBERTa-Base, and XLM-RoBERTa-Large) with two argument-ordering strategies and progressive unfreezing ratios to establish strong baselines. We then evaluate prompt-based large language models (namely Claude Opus 4.0) in zero-shot and few-shot settings to understand how LLMs respond to the newly proposed unified labels. Finally, we introduce HiDAC, a Hierarchical Dual-Adapter Contrastive learning model. Results show that while larger transformer models achieve higher accuracy, the improvements are modest, and that unfreezing the top 75% of encoder layers yields performance comparable to full fine-tuning while training far fewer parameters. Prompt-based models lag significantly behind fine-tuned transformers, and HiDAC achieves the highest overall accuracy (67.5%) while remaining more parameter-efficient than full fine-tuning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:59:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:59:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16900","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16900","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16900","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengsheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chengsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linhao","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Linhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijian","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhijian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ME-Mamba: Multi-Expert Mamba with Efficient Knowledge Capture and Fusion for Multimodal Survival Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T03:23:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:44:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Survival analysis using whole-slide images (WSIs) is crucial in cancer research. Despite significant successes, pathology images typically only provide slide-level labels, which hinders the learning of discriminative representations from gigapixel WSIs. With the rapid advancement of high-throughput sequencing technologies, multimodal survival analysis integrating pathology images and genomics data has emerged as a promising approach. We propose a Multi-Expert Mamba (ME-Mamba) system that captures discriminative pathological and genomic features while enabling efficient integration of both modalities. This approach achieves complementary information fusion without losing critical information from individual modalities, thereby facilitating accurate cancer survival analysis. Specifically, we first introduce a Pathology Expert and a Genomics Expert to process unimodal data separately. Both experts are designed with Mamba architectures that incorporate conventional scanning and attention-based scanning mechanisms, allowing them to extract discriminative features from long instance sequences containing substantial redundant or irrelevant information. Second, we design a Synergistic Expert responsible for modality fusion. It explicitly learns token-level local correspondences between the two modalities via Optimal Transport, and implicitly enhances distribution consistency through a global cross-modal fusion loss based on Maximum Mean Discrepancy. The fused feature representations are then passed to a mamba backbone for further integration. Through the collaboration of the Pathology Expert, Genomics Expert, and Synergistic Expert, our method achieves stable and accurate survival analysis with relatively low computational complexity. Extensive experimental results on five datasets in The Cancer Genome Atlas (TCGA) demonstrate our state-of-the-art performance."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16900","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:59:13Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:59:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16882","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16882","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16882","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhuo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junzhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiuze","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xiuze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xuming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamic Expert Specialization: Towards Catastrophic Forgetting-Free Multi-Domain MoE Adaptation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T02:30:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:43:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) models offer immense capacity via sparsely gated expert subnetworks, yet adapting them to multiple domains without catastrophic forgetting remains an open challenge. Existing approaches either incur prohibitive computation, suffer cross-domain interference, or require separate runs per domain. We propose DES-MoE, a dynamic expert specialization framework for multi-domain adaptation of Mixture-of-Experts models. DES-MoE addresses catastrophic forgetting through three innovations: (1) an adaptive router balancing pre-trained knowledge retention and task-specific updates via distillation, (2) real-time expert-domain correlation mapping to isolate domain-specific gradients, and (3) a three-phase adaptive fine-tuning schedule that progressively freezes non-specialized parameters. Evaluated on six domains (math, code, law, etc.), DES-MoE matches single-domain ESFT performance while training one unified model, reduces forgetting by 89% compared to full fine-tuning as domains scale from 2 to 6, and achieves 68% faster convergence than conventional methods. Our work establishes dynamic expert isolation as a scalable paradigm for multi-task MoE adaptation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16882","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:58:48Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:58:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:58:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16870","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16870","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16870","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chakkrit","familyName":"Tantithamthavorn","name":"Tantithamthavorn, Chakkrit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chetan","familyName":"Arora","name":"Arora, Chetan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gunel","familyName":"Gulmammadova","name":"Gulmammadova, Gunel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey","familyName":"Chua","name":"Chua, Joey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DecipherGuard: Understanding and Deciphering Jailbreak Prompts for a Safer Deployment of Intelligent Software Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-21T01:46:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:43:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Intelligent software systems powered by Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in critical sectors, raising concerns about their safety during runtime. Through an industry-academic collaboration when deploying an LLM-powered virtual customer assistant, a critical software engineering challenge emerged: how to enhance a safer deployment of LLM-powered software systems at runtime? While LlamaGuard, the current state-of-the-art runtime guardrail, offers protection against unsafe inputs, our study reveals a Defense Success Rate (DSR) drop of 24% under obfuscation- and template-based jailbreak attacks. In this paper, we propose DecipherGuard, a novel framework that integrates a deciphering layer to counter obfuscation-based prompts and a low-rank adaptation mechanism to enhance guardrail effectiveness against template-based attacks. Empirical evaluation on over 22,000 prompts demonstrates that DecipherGuard improves DSR by 36% to 65% and Overall Guardrail Performance (OGP) by 20% to 50% compared to LlamaGuard and two other runtime guardrails. These results highlight the effectiveness of DecipherGuard in defending LLM-powered software systems against jailbreak attacks during runtime."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under Review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16870","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:58:31Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:58:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:58:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16686","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16686","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16686","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengge","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Zhengge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowen","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Haowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EG-MLA: Embedding-Gated Multi-head Latent Attention for Scalable and Efficient LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T13:27:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:30:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reducing the key-value (KV) cache size is a crucial step toward enabling efficient inference in large language models (LLMs), especially under latency and memory constraints. While Multi-Head Attention (MHA) offers strong representational power, it incurs significant memory overhead. Recent work on Multi-head Latent Attention (MLA) mitigates this by compressing KV representations into a shared latent space, achieving a better trade-off between performance and cache efficiency. While MLA already achieves significant KV cache reduction, the scope for further compression remains limited without performance loss. In this paper, we propose \\textbf{Embedding-Gated Multi-head Latent Attention (EG-MLA)}, a novel extension of MLA that further reduces KV cache size while enhancing representational expressiveness. EG-MLA introduces a token-specific embedding gating mechanism applied in the latent space, enabling fine-grained modulation of compressed KV vectors with minimal additional computation. Compared to MHA, EG-MLA achieves over 91.6\\% reduction in KV cache size with negligible performance degradation. Relative to MLA, EG-MLA consistently improves task accuracy across diverse reasoning benchmarks while achieving up to 59.9\\% additional memory savings. Our theoretical analysis highlights how embedding gating induces implicit high-order interactions, and empirical evaluations demonstrate robust generalization across model scales and compression regimes. Notably, we successfully scale EG-MLA to over 1 billion parameters, demonstrating its practical viability for large-scale LLM deployment. These results establish EG-MLA as a memory- and compute-efficient attention mechanism that enables scalable, high-performance inference in modern LLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16686","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:54:14Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:54:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:54:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16670","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16670","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16670","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhuan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenhuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyue","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Wenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianguo","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jianguo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Speech-to-See: End-to-End Speech-Driven Open-Set Object Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T12:43:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:29:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Audio grounding, or speech-driven open-set object detection, aims to localize and identify objects directly from speech, enabling generalization beyond predefined categories. This task is crucial for applications like human-robot interaction where textual input is impractical. However, progress in this domain faces a fundamental bottleneck from the scarcity of large-scale, paired audio-image data, and is further constrained by previous methods that rely on indirect, text-mediated pipelines. In this paper, we introduce Speech-to-See (Speech2See), an end-to-end approach built on a pre-training and fine-tuning paradigm. Specifically, in the pre-training stage, we design a Query-Guided Semantic Aggregation module that employs learnable queries to condense redundant speech embeddings into compact semantic representations. During fine-tuning, we incorporate a parameter-efficient Mixture-of-LoRA-Experts (MoLE) architecture to achieve deeper and more nuanced cross-modal adaptation. Extensive experiments show that Speech2See achieves robust and adaptable performance across multiple benchmarks, demonstrating its strong generalization ability and broad applicability."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16670","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:53:51Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:53:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:53:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji Soo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Ji Soo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byungoh","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Byungoh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewon","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Jaewon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howoong","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Howoong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaewoon","familyName":"Byun","name":"Byun, Jaewoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunwoo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Captioning for Text-Video Retrieval via Dual-Group Direct Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T07:36:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:20:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In text-video retrieval, auxiliary captions are often used to enhance video understanding, bridging the gap between the modalities. While recent advances in multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have enabled strong zero-shot caption generation, we observe that such captions tend to be generic and indistinguishable across visually similar videos, limiting their utility for fine-grained retrieval. Moreover, conventional captioning approaches are typically evaluated using language generation metrics, such as BLEU, which are not typically tailored for retrieval tasks that require making discriminative distinctions between candidates. To address this, we propose $\\textbf{CaRe-DPO}$, a retrieval framework that directly optimizes caption generation using retrieval relevance scores. At its core is Dual-Group Direct Preference Optimization (DG-DPO), a novel learning strategy that supervises captioning by modeling preferences across groups of distinct video and caption pairs. In addition, we present an MLLM-based retrieval model that incorporates role-embeddings to better distinguish between textual inputs with different functional roles, such as an auxiliary caption and a text query. Through extensive experiments, we demonstrate that CaRe-DPO significantly enhances retrieval performance by effectively leveraging auxiliary knowledge to generate fine-grained captions for retrieval. Code is available at https://github.com/mlvlab/CaReDPO."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:51:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16475","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16475","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16475","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianchun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianci","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianci","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongzhe","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Rongzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Universal Debiasing for Language Models-based Tabular Data Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-20T00:06:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:12:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have achieved promising results in tabular data generation. However, inherent historical biases in tabular datasets often cause LLMs to exacerbate fairness issues, particularly when multiple advantaged and protected features are involved. In this work, we introduce a universal debiasing framework that minimizes group-level dependencies by simultaneously reducing the mutual information between advantaged and protected attributes. By leveraging the autoregressive structure and analytic sampling distributions of LLM-based tabular data generators, our approach efficiently computes mutual information, reducing the need for cumbersome numerical estimations. Building on this foundation, we propose two complementary methods: a direct preference optimization (DPO)-based strategy, namely UDF-DPO, that integrates seamlessly with existing models, and a targeted debiasing technique, namely UDF-MIX, that achieves debiasing without tuning the parameters of LLMs. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our framework effectively balances fairness and utility, offering a scalable and practical solution for debiasing in high-stakes applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16475","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:49:17Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:49:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:49:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16415","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16415","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16415","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengri","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhengri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Wen","name":"Wen, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Biao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"StereoAdapter: Adapting Stereo Depth Estimation to Underwater Scenes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-19T20:57:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:08:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Underwater stereo depth estimation provides accurate 3D geometry for robotics tasks such as navigation, inspection, and mapping, offering metric depth from low-cost passive cameras while avoiding the scale ambiguity of monocular methods. However, existing approaches face two critical challenges: (i) parameter-efficiently adapting large vision foundation encoders to the underwater domain without extensive labeled data, and (ii) tightly fusing globally coherent but scale-ambiguous monocular priors with locally metric yet photometrically fragile stereo correspondences. To address these challenges, we propose StereoAdapter, a parameter-efficient self-supervised framework that integrates a LoRA-adapted monocular foundation encoder with a recurrent stereo refinement module. We further introduce dynamic LoRA adaptation for efficient rank selection and pre-training on the synthetic UW-StereoDepth-40K dataset to enhance robustness under diverse underwater conditions. Comprehensive evaluations on both simulated and real-world benchmarks show improvements of 6.11% on TartanAir and 5.12% on SQUID compared to state-of-the-art methods, while real-world deployment with the BlueROV2 robot further demonstrates the consistent robustness of our approach. Code: https://github.com/AIGeeksGroup/StereoAdapter. Website: https://aigeeksgroup.github.io/StereoAdapter."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16415","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:47:52Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:47:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:47:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16275","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16275","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16275","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jugal","familyName":"Gajjar","name":"Gajjar, Jugal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kamalasankari","familyName":"Subramaniakuppusamy","name":"Subramaniakuppusamy, Kamalasankari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Relsy","familyName":"Puthal","name":"Puthal, Relsy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaustik","familyName":"Ranaware","name":"Ranaware, Kaustik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SecureFixAgent: A Hybrid LLM Agent for Automated Python Static Vulnerability Repair"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-18T15:45:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:01:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern software development pipelines face growing challenges in securing large codebases with extensive dependencies. Static analysis tools like Bandit are effective at vulnerability detection but suffer from high false positives and lack repair capabilities. Large Language Models (LLMs), in contrast, can suggest fixes but often hallucinate changes and lack self-validation. We present SecureFixAgent, a hybrid repair framework integrating Bandit with lightweight local LLMs (\u0026lt;8B parameters) in an iterative detect-repair-validate loop. To improve precision, we apply parameter-efficient LoRA-based fine-tuning on a diverse, curated dataset spanning multiple Python project domains, mitigating dataset bias and reducing unnecessary edits. SecureFixAgent uses Bandit for detection, the LLM for candidate fixes with explanations, and Bandit re-validation for verification, all executed locally to preserve privacy and reduce cloud reliance. Experiments show SecureFixAgent reduces false positives by 10.8% over static analysis, improves fix accuracy by 13.51%, and lowers false positives by 5.46% compared to pre-trained LLMs, typically converging within three iterations. Beyond metrics, developer studies rate explanation quality 4.5/5, highlighting its value for human trust and adoption. By combining verifiable security improvements with transparent rationale in a resource-efficient local framework, SecureFixAgent advances trustworthy, automated vulnerability remediation for modern pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 3 figures, 4 tables, 1 algorithm, accepted in the Robustness and Security of Large Language Models (ROSE-LLM) special session at ICMLA 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16275","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:44:35Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:44:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:44:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Jimin","familyName":"Roh","name":"Roh, Emily Jimin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojun","familyName":"Ahn","name":"Ahn, Hyojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel Yen-Chi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Samuel Yen-Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soohyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Soohyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joongheon","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Joongheon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"How Can Quantum Deep Learning Improve Large Language Models?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T08:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:01:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid progress of large language models (LLMs) has transformed natural language processing, yet the challenge of efficient adaptation remains unresolved. Full fine-tuning achieves strong performance but imposes prohibitive computational and memory costs. Parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) strategies, such as low-rank adaptation (LoRA), Prefix tuning, and sparse low-rank adaptation (SoRA), address this issue by reducing trainable parameters while maintaining competitive accuracy. However, these methods often encounter limitations in scalability, stability, and generalization across diverse tasks. Recent advances in quantum deep learning introduce novel opportunities through quantum-inspired encoding and parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs). In particular, the quantum-amplitude embedded adaptation (QAA) framework demonstrates expressive model updates with minimal overhead. This paper presents a systematic survey and comparative analysis of conventional PEFT methods and QAA. The analysis demonstrates trade-offs in convergence, efficiency, and representational capacity, while providing insight into the potential of quantum approaches for future LLM adaptation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:43:49Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:43:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:43:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.16206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.16206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junlin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Junlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Deep Reinforcement Learning in Factor Investment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-07-30T04:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T00:00:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Deep reinforcement learning has shown promise in trade execution, yet its use in low-frequency factor portfolio construction remains under-explored. A key obstacle is the high-dimensional, unbalanced state space created by stocks that enter and exit the investable universe. We introduce Conditional Auto-encoded Factor-based Portfolio Optimisation (CAFPO), which compresses stock-level returns into a small set of latent factors conditioned on 94 firm-specific characteristics. The factors feed a DRL agent implemented with both PPO and DDPG to generate continuous long-short weights. On 20 years of U.S. equity data (2000--2020), CAFPO outperforms equal-weight, value-weight, Markowitz, vanilla DRL, and Fama--French-driven DRL, delivering a 24.6\\% compound return and a Sharpe ratio of 0.94 out of sample. SHAP analysis further reveals economically intuitive factor attributions. Our results demonstrate that factor-aware representation learning can make DRL practical for institutional, low-turnover portfolio management."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.16206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-23T02:42:54Z","registered":"2025-09-23T02:42:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-23T02:42:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":41,"totalPages":2,"page":2},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=2\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=25\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-09-22T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-09-23T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28MoE+OR+MoEs+OR+PEFT+OR+DPO+OR+GRPO+OR+MLA+OR+SSM+OR+SSMs+OR+Mamba+OR+%22low-rank+adaptation%22+OR+%22low+rank+adaptation%22+OR+%22parameter-efficient%22+OR+%22parameter+efficient%22+OR+%22direct+preference+optimization%22+OR+%22group+relative+policy+optimization%22+OR+%22multi-head+latent+attention%22+OR+%22multihead+latent+attention%22+OR+%22state-space%22+OR+%22state+space%22%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28MoE+OR+MoEs+OR+PEFT+OR+DPO+OR+GRPO+OR+MLA+OR+SSM+OR+SSMs+OR+Mamba+OR+%22low-rank+adaptation%22+OR+%22low+rank+adaptation%22+OR+%22parameter-efficient%22+OR+%22parameter+efficient%22+OR+%22direct+preference+optimization%22+OR+%22group+relative+policy+optimization%22+OR+%22multi-head+latent+attention%22+OR+%22multihead+latent+attention%22+OR+%22state-space%22+OR+%22state+space%22%29%29+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28neural+OR+learning+OR+training+OR+inference+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs%29+OR+descriptions.description%3A%28neural+OR+learning+OR+training+OR+inference+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs%29%29"}}